Genealogy of Jesus
This is the genealogy of Jesus Christ: Jesus came from the lineage of David, and David from the lineage of Abraham.
Ibrahim is the father of Ishaq. Ishaq is the father of Yaqub. Yaqub is the father of Yahuda and his brothers.
Judah, father of Perez and Zerah, whose mother was Tamar. Perez, father of Hezron. Hezron, father of Aram.
Aram, father of Aminadab. Aminadab, father of Nahshon. Nahshon, father of Salmon.
Salmon, father of Boaz, whose mother was Rahab. Boaz, father of Obed, whose mother was Ruth. Obed, father of Jesse.
King Abu Dawood. Dawood Abu Suleiman, whose mother was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon son of Rehoboam. Rehoboam son of Abijah. Abijah son of Asa.
Asa, father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat, father of Joram. Joram, father of Ahaziah.
Azariah, father of Jotham. Jotham, father of Ahaz. Ahaz, father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah, father of Menasseh. Menasseh, father of Amon. Amon, father of Josiah.
Jehoiachin #Yeknia. 11:1 Another name for Jehoiachin. And his brothers. This is until the time of the exile of the people of Israel to Babylon.
After the exile to Babylon: Jeconiah father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel, father of Abiud. Abiud, father of Eliakim. Eliakim, father of Azor.
Azour Abu Sadouq. Sadouq Abu Akheem. Akheem Abu Alyoud.
Eliud, father of Lazarus. Lazarus, father of Mattan. Mattan, father of Jacob.
Jacob, son of Joseph, husband of Mary. And Mary is the mother of Jesus, who is called "the Christ."
There are fourteen generations from Abraham to David, fourteen generations from David to the exile, and fourteen generations from the exile to Christ. The birth of Jesus Christ
The birth of Jesus Christ took place as follows: His mother Mary was betrothed to a man named Joseph. But before they married, she found out that she was pregnant by the power of the Holy Spirit.
But Joseph was a righteous man, and he did not want to expose her secret, so he decided to leave her quietly.
And while Joseph was thinking about this, an angel appeared to him in a dream and said to him: "Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife, because the child she is pregnant with is from the Holy Spirit.
She will give birth to a son, and you will name him Jesus, because he will save his people from their sins.»
This happened to fulfill what the Lord had spoken through the prophet:
Behold, the virgin shall conceive and bear a son, and his name shall be called ‹Immanuel›, which means: ‹God with us.›
And when Joseph woke up from his sleep, he did everything the angel of the Lord had commanded him, and took his wife to his home.
But he did not know her until she gave birth to the child whom he named "Jesus."
Wise Men from the East
When Jesus was born in the town of Bethlehem in the region of Judea during the reign of King Herod, some wise men from the East came to the city of Jerusalem,
And they asked, "Where is the child who has been born king of the Jews? For we saw his star in the East, and we have come to worship him."
King Herod was disturbed when he heard that, as were all the inhabitants of the city of Jerusalem.
Herod gathered all the chief priests and teachers of the law and asked them where the Messiah was to be born.
So they said to him: "In the city of Bethlehem in the region of Judea, because this is what is written by the prophet:
"You, Bethlehem, located in the land of Judah, are by no means insignificant among the rulers of Judah, for from you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel."
Herod summoned the wise men and met with them secretly, and learned from them the exact time when the star appeared,
Then he sent them to the city of Bethlehem and said, "Go and search for the child. And when you find him, let me know, so that I may also come and worship him."
So the wise men listened to the king and then went away. And behold, the star they had seen in the East went before them until it came and stood over the place where the child was.
So they rejoiced with great joy when they saw the star.
So they entered the house and saw the child with his mother Mary, and they knelt on the ground, prostrating to him. Then they opened their treasure chests and presented him with gifts: gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
Then God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod, so they traveled to their country by another route. The escape to Egypt
After the wise men had traveled, the angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream and said to him: "Get up, take the child and his mother, and flee to Egypt, and stay there until I tell you, because Herod will search for the child to kill him."
Joseph got up, took the child and his mother at night, and went to Egypt.
And he stayed there until the death of Herod. This happened to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet: "Out of Egypt I called my son." #Out of … my son. 15:2 From the book of Hosea 11:1. Herod kills the children of Bethlehem
When Herod learned that the wise men had deceived him, he became very angry and ordered the killing of all the boys in the city of Bethlehem and the surrounding area, aged two years and under, according to the time confirmed to him by the wise men.
Then what was said on the tongue of the prophet Jeremiah came to pass:
A voice was heard in Ramah, a voice of weeping and great mourning. It is the voice of Rachel weeping for her children, refusing to be comforted because they are dead. The return from Egypt
After the death of Herod, the angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream in Egypt.
And he said to him: "Get up, take the child and his mother, and return to the land of Israel, for those who were trying to kill the child are dead."
So Joseph got up, took the child and his mother, and went to the land of Israel.
But when he heard that Archelaus had become king in the region of Judea in place of his father Herod, he was afraid to go there. After God warned him in a dream, he went to the region of Galilee,
And he lived in a town called Nazareth. This happened to fulfill what the prophets said, that the Messiah would be called a Nazarene. #Nazareth. 23:2 Relating to the city of Nazareth. It is also a word similar to the Hebrew word meaning "branch" found in Isaiah 11:1, referring to the promise of the coming of the Messiah from the lineage of David.
John the Baptist
In those days, John the Baptist came preaching in the wilderness of Judea,
And he was saying, "Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is near."
And this is the John who was spoken of by the prophet Isaiah when he said: "A voice of one calling in the wilderness, 'Prepare the way for the Lord, make straight paths for him.'"
John was wearing clothes made of camel hair, with a leather belt around his waist, and he ate locusts and wild honey.
At that time, all the people began to come from the city of Jerusalem and from all Judea, and from the region around the Jordan River to hear him.
And he would baptize them in the Jordan River after they confessed their sins.
And when John saw many of the Pharisees and Sadducees coming to be baptized by him, he said to them, "You brood of vipers, who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?
Produce fruit that proves your repentance,
And do not boast, saying: "Abraham is our father." For I say to you that God is able to create children for Abraham from these stones.
Here is the axe laid at the roots of the tree trunks. Every tree that does not bear good fruit will be cut down and thrown into the fire.
"I baptize you with water for the repentance of your sins; but he who comes after me is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to carry. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire."
He will carry his grains in his hand and winnow with his fan, gathering his wheat into the barn and burning the chaff with unquenchable fire.» The Baptism of Jesus
Then Jesus came from the region of Galilee to the Jordan River, because he wanted John to baptize him.
But John tried to stop him and said, "I need to be baptized by you, so why are you coming to me?"
Jesus answered him, "Allow it now, for it is proper for us to fulfill all that God requires." Then John allowed him to be baptized.
Jesus deliberately immersed in the water. As he rose from the water, the heavens opened, and he saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove and resting upon him.
And a voice came from heaven saying, "You are my beloved Son; I am well pleased with you."
The Experience of Jesus
And the Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil.
After Jesus had fasted for forty days and forty nights, he became hungry.
Then the tempter came to him #the tempter. 3:4 That is, Satan. And said to him: "If you are the Son of God, tell these stones to become loaves of bread."
But Jesus answered him, "It is written: 'Man shall not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes from the mouth of God.'"
Then Satan took him to the holy city and stood him on the pinnacle of the temple.
And he said to him: "If you are truly the Son of God, throw yourself down from here. For the scripture says: 'God will command his angels concerning you.' 'And they will: "They will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone."' "
Jesus answered him, "The Scripture also says: 'Do not test the Lord your God.'"
Then Satan took him to a high mountain and showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory before his eyes.
Then he said to him: "I will give you all of this if you bow down to me."
Then Jesus said to him, "Away from me, Satan! For it is written: 'You shall worship the Lord your God, and him only shall you serve.'"
Then Satan left him, and the angels came and attended him. Jesus begins his ministry in Galilee
When Jesus heard that John had been arrested, he withdrew to Galilee.
But he did not stay in Nazareth; rather, he went and lived in the city of Capernaum near the Sea of Galilee in the regions of Zebulun and Naphtali.
This happened to fulfill what God said through the prophet Isaiah:
The land of Zebulun and Naphtali, the way of the sea, beyond the Jordan, the land of Galilee, where the nations dwell.
The people who walked in darkness have seen a great light; those who dwelt in the land of the shadow of death, upon them a light has shined.
From that time, Jesus began to preach and say: "Repent, for the kingdom of heaven has come near." Jesus chooses some of his disciples
While Jesus was walking along the shore of the Sea of Galilee, he saw two brothers, Simon who is also called Peter, and his brother Andrew, casting a net into the lake, for they were fishermen.
He said to them, "Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men."
They immediately left their nets and followed him.
Then Jesus went from there and saw two other brothers, James son of Zebedee and his brother John. He saw them in the boat with their father Zebedee, mending their fishing nets, and he called them.
They immediately left the boat and their father and followed him. Jesus teaches and heals
And Jesus traveled throughout all the region of Galilee, teaching the people in their synagogues and proclaiming the good news of the kingdom of God. He healed every disease and sickness among the people.
News about him spread throughout all the lands of Syria, so they brought to him all the sick suffering from various diseases and pains, those possessed by evil spirits, and those afflicted with epilepsy and paralysis, and Jesus healed them.
He was followed by large crowds of people who came from the region of Galilee, from the Ten Cities, from Jerusalem, from the region of Judea, and from the eastern side of the Jordan River.
The Teaching of Jesus
And when Jesus saw the crowds, he went up on a hill and sat down. Then his disciples came to him,
And he began to speak and teach them, saying:
Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are those who mourn, for God will comfort them.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth. #inherit the earth. 5:5 See Psalm 37:11. It may here mean a future spiritual inheritance.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst to do the will of God, #to do the will of God. 6:5 Literally: "to righteousness." Because God will satisfy them.
Blessed are the merciful, for God will have mercy on them.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who are persecuted for righteousness' sake, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are you when people insult you, persecute you, and falsely say all kinds of evil against you because you are my disciples.
Rejoice and be glad, because your reward will be great in heaven. For in the same way they persecuted the prophets who lived before you. Salt and Light
You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its flavor, how can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything, except to be thrown out and trampled underfoot.
You are the light of the world. A city built on a hill cannot be hidden,
No one lights a lamp and puts it under a bowl! Instead, they put it on a high stand so that it shines on everyone in the house.
In the same way, let your light shine before others, that they may see your good deeds and glorify your Father in heaven." Jesus and the Law of Moses
"Do not think that I have come to abolish the law of Moses or the teachings of the prophets. I have not come to abolish them, but to give them their full meaning."
I tell you the truth, until heaven and earth pass away, not the smallest letter or stroke shall pass from the law until all is accomplished.
Therefore, whoever breaks the least of these commandments and teaches others to do the same will be considered the least in the kingdom of heaven. But whoever obeys these commandments and teaches others to obey them will be considered the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
For I tell you, unless your obedience to God exceeds your obedience to the teachers of the law and the Pharisees, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven. Anger
You know that it was said to your ancestors: "Do not kill. #Do not kill. 21:5 From the Book of Exodus 20:13, and the Book of Deuteronomy 5:17. And anyone who kills deserves to be tried."
As for me, I tell you that whoever gets angry at another person deserves to be tried, and whoever insults another person should stand before the court. And whoever says to another person: "You fool" deserves hell.
So if you are offering a gift at the altar, and there you remember that someone else has something against you,
Leave your offering there in front of the altar, go and be reconciled with that person first, then come back and present your offering.
Quickly defeat your opponent while walking with him to the court. Otherwise, he will hand you over to the judge, and the judge will hand you over to the jailer who will throw you into prison.
I tell you the truth, you will not get out of there until you have spent your last penny. Adultery
You have heard that it was said: "Do not commit adultery." #Do not commit adultery. 27:5 From the Book of Exodus 20:14, and the Book of Deuteronomy 5:18.
But I say to you: Everyone who looks at a woman with lust has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
Therefore, if your right eye causes you to sin, pluck it out and throw it away from you. It is better for you to lose one part of your body than for your whole body to be thrown into hell.
And if your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away from you. It is better for you to lose one part of your body than for your whole body to be thrown into hell. Divorce
It was also said: "If a man divorces his wife, he should give her a certificate proving that." #If he divorces … that. 31:5 From the book of Deuteronomy 24:1.
As for me, I say to you that whoever divorces his wife, except for the reason of adultery, causes her to commit adultery. And whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. The oath.
You have also heard that it was said to your ancestors: "Do not swear falsely, but fulfill what you have sworn to do to the Lord." #Do not break your oath … to the Lord. 33:5 See Leviticus 19:12, Numbers 30:2, and Deuteronomy 23:21.
But I say to you: Do not swear at all.
Do not swear by the sky, for it is the throne of God, nor by the earth, for it is the footstool of His feet, #the footstool of His feet. 35:5 meaning it belongs to Him and is under His authority. Nor by the city of Jerusalem, for it is the city of the great King.
Do not swear by your head, for you cannot make a single hair black or white.
If you want to say "yes," then say "yes." And if you want to say "no," then say "no." And anything beyond that is from the evil one. #TheEvilOne. 37:5 Satan. Resisting evil
You have heard that it was said: "An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth." #An eye … for a tooth. 38:5 From the Book of Exodus 21:24, and the Book of Leviticus 24:20.
As for me, I say: Do not resist evil. But if someone strikes you on your right cheek, offer him also the other cheek.
And if anyone wants to sue you to take your shirt, let him have your coat as well.
And if anyone forces you to go one mile, go with him two miles.
If someone asks you for something, give it to them. Do not refuse to lend to anyone who asks to borrow from you. Love for all.
You have heard that it was said: "Love your neighbor and hate your enemy." #Love … your enemy. 43:5 From the book of Leviticus 19:18.
But I say to you: Love your enemies, and pray for those who persecute you,
So you will be children of your Father in heaven. For God causes the sun to rise on the wicked and the righteous, and sends rain on the just and the unjust.
If you only love those who love you, what reward do you deserve? Do not even tax collectors do the same?
And if you only greet your brothers, what distinguishes you from others? Do not even the idolaters do the same?
Therefore, be perfect, just as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Giving
Beware of giving your alms before others to be seen by them; otherwise, your Father who is in heaven will not reward you.
When you give to the needy, do not announce it as if you are blowing a trumpet like hypocrites do in gatherings and streets seeking people's praise. I tell you the truth, they have received their full reward for that.
But when you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
So that your giving may be in secret. And your Father who sees what is done in secret will reward you. Prayer
And when you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to pray standing in the synagogues and on the street corners to be seen by others. Truly I tell you, they have received their full reward.
But when you pray, go into your room and close your door, and pray to your Father who is unseen. And your Father, who sees what is done in secret, will reward you.
And when you pray, do not utter words without understanding as the idolaters do; they think that their prayers will be answered because of their many words.
So do not be like them, for your Father knows what you need before you ask Him.
Therefore, pray as follows: ‹Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name,
Let your kingdom come, your will be done, here on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us today our daily bread,
And forgive us our sins, as we also have forgiven those who wrong us.
And do not lead us into temptation, but deliver us from the evil one. #TheEvilOne. 13:6 The Satan (Iblis). For yours is the kingdom and the power and the glory, forever and ever. Amen.
For if you forgive others their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive others their trespasses, your Father will not forgive your trespasses. Fasting
And when you fast, do not be like the hypocrites who show a sad countenance. For they disfigure their faces so that they may appear to men to be fasting. Truly, I say to you, they have received their reward in full.
But when you fast, anoint your head with oil, and wash your face,
So that people do not notice that you are fasting. Your Father, whom you do not see, sees it. Then your Father, who sees what happens in secret, will reward you. God, not money.
Do not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.
But store up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where moth and rust do not destroy, and where thieves do not break in and steal.
For where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.
The lamp of the body is the eye. If your eyes are healthy, then your whole body will be filled with light.
But if your eye is evil, your whole body will be full of darkness. If the light within you is actually darkness, how great is that darkness?
No one can serve two masters. Either they will hate one and love the other, or they will be devoted to one and despise the other. You cannot serve both God and wealth. #wealth. 24:6 Literally "Mamona." It is an Aramaic word meaning "wealth," in its negative sense, as it represents here a god served by people instead of God. The kingdom of God first.
Therefore I tell you, do not worry about your life, what you will eat or drink. And do not worry about your body, what you will wear. For life is more important than food, and the body is more important than clothing.
Look at the birds of the sky; they do not sow or reap, nor do they gather into barns, yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not more valuable to God than the birds?
Which one of you can add an hour to his life by worrying?
And why do you worry about what you will wear? Look at how the lilies of the field grow. They do not toil or spin.
But I tell you, not even Solomon in all his glory was dressed like one of these.
If God clothes the grass of the fields, which you see here today and tomorrow is thrown into the oven, will He not care for you even more, O you of little faith?
So do not worry and do not ask yourselves: "What shall we eat?" or "What shall we drink?" or "What shall we wear?"
These are matters sought after by the people of the other world, and your Heavenly Father knows that you need them all.
But seek first the kingdom of God and His righteousness, and all these things will be given to you as well.
Do not worry about tomorrow, for each day has enough troubles of its own, and tomorrow will have its own troubles.
Judging others
"Do not judge others, so that God will not judge you."
For with the judgment you pronounce you will be judged, and with the measure you use it will be measured to you.
Why do you see the speck of dust in your brother's eye but do not notice the large plank in your own eye?
And how can you say to your brother, "Let me take the speck out of your eye," when there is a plank in your own eye?
O hypocrite! First take the plank out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to take the speck out of your brother's eye.
Do not give what is sacred to dogs, nor throw your pearls before pigs! For pigs trample them with their feet, and the dogs turn to you and tear you apart. Perseverance in seeking
Ask and it will be given to you; seek and you will find; knock and the door will be opened to you.
For everyone who asks receives, and the one who seeks finds, and to the one who knocks it will be opened.
Which of you, if your son asks for a loaf of bread, will give him a stone?
Or if he asks for a fish, will he give him a snake?
You, despite your wickedness, know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will the Father in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him? The Golden Rule
"So in the same way that you want others to treat you, you should treat them. This is the essence of the law of Moses and the teachings of the prophets. The way to heaven and the way to hell."
Enter through the narrow gate that leads to heaven. For the gate that leads to destruction is wide, and the way is easy, and many enter through it.
But the gate that leads to life is very narrow, and the road to it is full of difficulties, and only a few find this path. A warning against false teachings
Beware of false prophets who come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravenous wolves.
You will know them by their deeds. For people do not gather grapes from thornbushes, nor figs from thistles!
Likewise, every good tree bears good fruit, and every bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree cannot bear bad fruit, nor can a bad tree bear good fruit.
And every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
Therefore, you will recognize false prophets by their fruits.
"Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven."
Many will say to me on that last day: "Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not perform many miracles in your name?"
Then I will say to them clearly: I never knew you. Depart from me, you workers of evil. The smart man and the foolish man
"Everyone who listens to these teachings of mine and acts on them is like a wise man who built his house on the rock."
The rain fell, the floodwaters rose, the wind blew and struck that house, but it did not fall because its foundation was on the rock.
And everyone who hears these words of mine and does not put them into practice is like a foolish man who built his house on the sand.
Then the rain fell, the floodwaters rose, the wind blew and struck that house, and it collapsed with a tremendous fall!»
And when Jesus had finished saying these things, the people were amazed at his teaching,
Because he taught them with authority, not like the teachers of the law.
Jesus cleanses a leper
Then Jesus came down from the mountainous region, and many followed him.
A man with leprosy came to him, bowed before him, and said: "O Master, you are able to make me clean, if you wish."
Then Jesus reached out his hand and touched him, saying, "Yes, I am willing; be clean." Immediately his leprosy was cleansed.
Then Jesus said to him, "Be careful not to tell anyone what happened to you, but go and show yourself to the priest, #go … to the priest. 4:8 The priest was the one who decided according to the law when a leper was considered clean. And offer the sacrifice that Moses commanded, so that people may know that you have been healed." Faith of a Roman officer
And Jesus entered the city of Capernaum, and a Roman officer came to him #Roman officer. 5:8 literally "centurion." Repeated in verses 8, 13.
He said: "Sir, my servant is very ill and bedridden at home. He is motionless and suffering from severe pain."
Jesus said to him, "I will go and heal him."
The officer replied to him: "Sir, I do not deserve for you to enter my house; all you have to do is say a word and my servant will be healed."
I myself am a man under authority, and I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to this soldier, "Go!" and he goes. I say to another, "Come!" and he comes. I say to my servant, "Do this!" and he does it."
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed and said to those who were following him, "Truly I tell you, I have not found such faith even in Israel."
I tell you that many will come from the east and the west and will take their places at the feast with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob in the kingdom of heaven.
But those who were supposed to inherit the kingdom will be thrown into the outer darkness. There people will weep and gnash their teeth!"
Then Jesus said to the officer, "Go, and let it be as you have believed." And the servant of that officer was healed at that very moment. "He bore our diseases"
And when Jesus came to Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law lying in bed, and her fever was very high.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her; she got up and began to serve him.
And that evening, people brought to him many individuals possessed by evil spirits, so he cast out the spirits by the command of his mouth, and healed all the sick.
This happened to fulfill what God said through the prophet Isaiah: "He took up our infirmities and carried our diseases." Following Jesus
And when Jesus saw many people around him, he commanded his disciples to go to the opposite side of the lake.
Then one of the teachers of the law came to him and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
And Jesus said to him, "Foxes have dens, and birds of the sky have nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head."
And another of his disciples said to him, "Lord, allow me to wait until I bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow me, and let the dead bury their own dead." Jesus calms the storm
And Jesus got into the boat, and his disciples followed him.
Then a fierce storm arose on the lake, so much so that the boat was covered by the waves. But Jesus was sleeping.
They came to him and woke him, saying, "Lord, save us, we are perishing."
Then Jesus said to them, "Why are you afraid, you of little faith?" Then he got up and rebuked the winds and the lake, and there was a great calm.
But they were amazed and said, "What kind of man is this, that even the winds and the sea obey him?" Jesus casts out evil spirits from two men.
Then Jesus arrived at the region of the Gadarenes on the other side of the lake, and two men possessed by evil spirits came out of the tombs to meet him. The men were dangerous, so no one dared to travel that road.
The evil spirits cried out: "What do you want from us, Son of God? Have you come here to torment us before the appointed time?"
There was a large herd of pigs grazing at a distance from them.
The evil spirits begged him, saying, "If you cast us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Then Jesus said, "Go." So the evil spirits came out of the two men and entered the pigs. At that moment, the whole herd rushed down the steep bank into the lake and the pigs drowned in the water.
The shepherds fled to the town and told the people what had happened to those possessed by evil spirits.
All the townspeople came out to see Jesus. And when they saw him, they begged him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a paralyzed man
Jesus got into a boat to cross to the other side of the lake, and he arrived at his town.
Some people brought to him a paralyzed man lying on his bed. When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralyzed man, "Take heart, son; your sins are forgiven."
Some Islamic law teachers began to say: "This man is insulting God with his words."
Jesus knew what they were thinking and said to them, "Why are you thinking evil thoughts in your hearts?"
Which of the two is easier: to say, "Your sins are forgiven," or to say, "Get up and walk"?
But I will show you that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then he said to the paralyzed man, "Get up, take your mat, and go home!"
He got up and went to his house.
And when the people saw this, they were filled with awe and glorified God because He had given such authority to men. When will Jesus be followed?
As Jesus was passing by, he saw a man named Matthew sitting at the tax collector's place. Jesus said to him, "Follow me!" So he got up and followed him.
While he was eating at Matthew's house, many tax collectors and sinners came and ate with Jesus and his disciples.
When the Pharisees saw this, they said to his disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
When Jesus heard that, he said, "It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick.
So go and understand what the scripture means when it says: "I desire mercy, not animal sacrifices." #I desire … animal. 13:9 From the book of Hosea 6:6. "I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners." A question about fasting
Then the disciples of John came to him and asked, "Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but your disciples do not fast?"
Then Jesus said to them, "Can the wedding guests mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? But the days will come when the bridegroom will be taken away from them, and then they will fast.
No one patches an old garment with a piece of new cloth, because the new cloth will shrink and tear the old garment, making the hole worse.
No one puts new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the skins will burst, the wine will run out, and the skins will be ruined. Instead, new wine is put into new wineskins, and both are preserved.» Jesus raises a dead girl // and heals a bleeding woman
While Jesus was speaking, a synagogue leader came to him, bowed before him, and said, "My daughter has just died, but come and lay your hand on her, and she will live."
Then Jesus got up and went with the chief priest, and his disciples followed him.
There was a woman who had suffered from severe bleeding for twelve years. She came up behind Jesus and touched the edge of his cloak.
She said in her heart: "If only I touch his cloak, I will be healed."
Jesus turned around, saw her, and said to her, "Take heart, daughter; your faith has healed you." And the woman was healed completely at that moment.
And when Jesus arrived at the house of the synagogue leader, he saw those playing funeral tunes, and the people were in chaos.
He said: "Go outside. The boy is not dead, he is just sleeping." They laughed at him.
And when the people had gone out of the house, Jesus entered the girl's room and took her hand, and she got up.
News about Jesus spread throughout the whole region. Jesus heals the blind // and casts out an evil spirit
And after Jesus left that place, two blind men followed him, crying out, "Son of David, have mercy on us."
And when Jesus entered the house, the two blind men came to him, and Jesus said to them, "Do you believe that I can heal you?" They answered him, "Yes, Lord."
Then he touched their eyes and said, "Let it be for you as you believed."
Then the two blind men regained their sight. Jesus warned them sternly, saying, "Do not let anyone know what happened to you."
But they went and spread the news throughout that entire area.
And while they were going, some people brought to Jesus a mute man, because he was possessed by evil spirits.
Then Jesus cast out the unclean spirit, and the mute man began to speak. The people were amazed and said, "Nothing like this has ever been seen in Israel before."
But the Pharisees began to say, "He casts out demons by the power of Beelzebul, #Beelzebul. 34:9 One of the names of the devil. The prince of those spirits." The harvest is plentiful.
And Jesus went through all the towns and villages, teaching in their synagogues, proclaiming the good news of the kingdom. He healed every disease and sickness among the people.
When Jesus saw the crowds, he had compassion on them, because they were distressed and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "The harvest is plentiful, but the workers are few."
Pray to the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into his harvest.
Jesus sends his disciples
Jesus called his twelve disciples and gave them authority over unclean spirits, to cast them out and to heal every disease and sickness.
And these are the names of the twelve apostles: first, Simon who is also called Peter, and his brother Andrew, James son of Zebedee and his brother John,
Philips and Bartholomew, Thomas and Matthew the tax collector, James son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus,
Simon, who is also called "the Zealot," #the Zealot. 4:10 From a Jewish political party resisting Roman rule, called the "Zealots." And Judas Iscariot, who betrayed Jesus.
Jesus sent out the twelve and gave them the following instructions: "Do not go to a non-Jewish area, nor enter a Samaritan city,
But go to the lost sheep of the children of Israel,
And they proclaimed that the kingdom of heaven is near.
Heal the sick, raise the dead, cleanse those with leprosy, cast out demons. You received authority to do this without payment; give to others without payment as well.
Do not carry gold, silver, or copper coins in your belts,
Do not carry a bag, extra clothes, extra shoes, or a cane. For the worker deserves his food.
And when you enter any city or village, look for a person worthy of receiving you, and stay with him until you leave the city.
And when you enter a house, greet its inhabitants with peace.
If the people of the house are deserving, then your peace will reach them. And if they are not deserving, then your peace will return to you.
If anyone does not welcome you into a house or city, and does not listen to your message, leave that house or city, and shake the dust off your feet.
I tell you the truth, on the day of judgment, the people of Sodom and Gomorrah will have a more bearable fate than that city. Jesus warns of tribulations.
"Behold, I send you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be as shrewd as snakes and as innocent as doves."
Beware of people, for they will hand you over to be judged in their courts, and they will flog you in their synagogues.
They will hand you over to governors and kings because you are my disciples. This will be your opportunity to testify about me to Jews and to Gentiles.
And when they arrest you, do not worry about what you will say, because you will be given the right words at that hour.
Remember that it is not you who will speak, but the Spirit of your Father who will speak through you.
Brother will deliver his brother to death, and a father his child. Children will rise against their parents and kill them.
And all people will hate you because of my name, but the one who remains faithful to the end will be saved.
And when they persecute you in one city, flee to another. Truly, I say to you, you will not finish going through all the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes again.
No student is better than his teacher, nor is any servant greater than his master.
Let the student be content to become like his teacher, and let the servant be content to become like his master. If the head of the house is called "Beelzebul," #Beelzebul. 25:10 One of the names of the devil. A reference to Matthew 9:34. » then what will the rest of the members of the house be called? Fear God, not people.
So do not fear them; there is nothing hidden except that it will be revealed, and nothing concealed except that it will be made known.
So whatever I tell you in the dark, speak in the light; and whatever you hear whispered in the ear, proclaim it from the rooftops.
"Do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul; rather, fear Him who can destroy both soul and body in hell."
Are not two sparrows sold for a penny? Yet not one of them will fall to the ground without your Father's permission.
As for you, even the hairs on your head are all counted.
So do not be afraid; you are worth more than many sparrows. Confessing Christ before people
"Whoever acknowledges me before others, I will also acknowledge before my Father in heaven."
And whoever denies me before others, I will also deny before my Father in heaven. Christ first.
"Do not think that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword!"
I have come: ‹For a man will be divided against his father, a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law.
So the enemies of a person are his own family!
"Whoever loves father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. Whoever loves son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me."
And whoever does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever tries to gain their life will lose it, but whoever loses their life for my sake will find it.
Whoever welcomes you welcomes me, and whoever welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me.
Whoever welcomes a prophet because he is a prophet will receive the reward of a prophet. And whoever welcomes a righteous person because he is righteous will receive the reward of a righteous person.
And whoever gives even a cup of cold water to one of my humble disciples, because he is my disciple, truly I tell you, he will not lose his reward.
John the Baptist and not Jesus
And when Jesus finished teaching his twelve disciples, he left that place and went to teach and proclaim his message in the region of Galilee. Jesus and John the Baptist
While John the Baptist was in prison, he heard about everything that Christ was doing, so he sent a message with some of his disciples.
And he asked him, "Are you the one we are waiting for, or should we wait for another?"
And Jesus answered them, saying, "Go and tell John what you have heard and seen:
Now the blind see, the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead are raised, and the poor hear the good news.
And congratulations to those who do not hesitate to believe in me.»
And as John's disciples left the place, Jesus began to speak to the people about John, saying: "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
Why did you go out then? To see a man dressed in fine clothes? Those who wear fine clothes live in the palaces of kings.
So why did you go out then? To see a prophet? He is indeed one. But I tell you that you have seen someone greater than a prophet!
This is what was written about him: "Behold, I send my messenger before you, to prepare the way before you."
I tell you the truth, among those born of women there has not risen anyone greater than John the Baptist. Yet the one who is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.
From the time of John the Baptist until now, the kingdom has been facing violent attacks, and the violent try to seize it by force.
For the law of Moses and all the prophets prophesied until the time of John.
If you want to accept what the prophets and the law say, then John is Elijah whose coming they predicted. #Elijah … his coming. 14:11 Elijah was one of God's prophets around 850 BC. The Jews were expecting his coming based on Malachi 4:5-6.
Whoever has ears, let them hear.
What is this generation like? It is like children sitting in the markets, calling out to their friends and saying:
‹We sang for you, but you did not dance. We sang funeral songs for you, but you did not wail!›
John came, who did not eat like others and did not drink wine like others, and the people said about him: "He has an evil spirit."
Then came the Son of Man who eats and drinks wine like others, and people said about him: "Look at this man, he is gluttonous and drunk, and he is a friend of tax collectors and sinners!" But the fruits of wisdom are what prove that it is true wisdom." Jesus warns the sinful cities
Then Jesus began to rebuke the cities where he had performed most of his miracles, because their inhabitants did not repent of their sins.
He said: "Woe to you, Korzin! Woe to you, house of Sidon! For if the miracles that occurred in you had occurred in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago, and their people would have put on sackcloth and sat in ashes.
But I tell you that the condition of the people of Tyre and Sidon will be easier than your condition on the Day of Judgment.
And you, Capernaum, do you imagine that you will be lifted up to heaven? No, but you will be brought down to the abyss! For if the miracles that occurred in you had occurred in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.
But I tell you that the condition of the people of Sodom will be more bearable than that of your people on the day of judgment.» Jesus is the source of comfort
At that time, Jesus spoke and said: "I thank you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth. For you have hidden these things from the wise and the intelligent, and revealed them to the simple, like children.
Yes, father, because you were pleased with doing this.»
«All things have been handed over to me by the Father. No one knows the Son except the Father, and no one knows the Father except the Son and anyone to whom the Son chooses to reveal him.»
Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Carry my yoke #yoke. 29:11 The yoke is a wooden tool placed on animals to help them carry heavy loads, and it is a Jewish symbol for the law. Take my yoke upon you, and learn from me, for I am gentle and humble in heart, and you will find rest for your souls.
"For the yoke that I give is easy, and the burden that I lay on you is light."
Jesus: Lord of the Sabbath
At that time, Jesus went to walk in the fields on a Sabbath day. His disciples became hungry and began to pick some heads of grain and eat them.
But when the Pharisees saw it, they said to him, "Look, your disciples are doing what is not lawful to do on the Sabbath."
Then Jesus said to them, "Have you not read in the Scriptures what David did when he and those with him were hungry?"
He entered the house of God and ate some of the consecrated bread, even though it was not lawful for him or those with him to eat that bread; it is only permitted for the priests alone.
Have you not also read in the law of Moses how the priests on the Sabbath do not keep the law concerning the Sabbath? Yet they are not held accountable for their actions,
But I tell you that there is something greater than the temple here.
If you only knew what the book means when it says: "I desire mercy, not animal sacrifices." #I desire … animal. 7:12 From the book of Hosea 6:6. When you judged those innocent ones.
"For the Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath." Healing on the Sabbath day
Then he left that place and went to their complex.
There was a man with a paralyzed hand. The people asked Jesus, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?" They asked him this to have a basis for accusing him.
He said to them: "Suppose one of you has a sheep that falls into a pit on the Sabbath, wouldn't you hold onto it and take it out?
And human beings are more important than sheep. So if good deeds are done on Saturday, they are in accordance with the law.»
Then Jesus said to the man with the withered hand, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and it was completely restored, just like his other hand.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot how they might kill Jesus. Jesus: The Chosen Servant of God
Jesus knew their plan, and left that place. Large crowds followed him, and he healed them all,
And he ordered them not to reveal who he was.
This happened to fulfill what God said through the prophet Isaiah:
Behold my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved in whom my soul delights. I will put my Spirit upon him, and he will bring justice to the nations.
He will not quarrel with anyone, will not shout, and no one will hear his voice in their streets.
He will not break even the bent reed, nor extinguish even the smoking wick. And he will continue until justice prevails.
And all nations will put their hope in him." Jesus the Sultan
Then they brought to him a man who was blind and mute because he was possessed by an evil spirit, and he healed him. So the mute man began to speak and see.
The people were amazed and said, "Could this man be the son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This man casts out demons by the power of Beelzebul #Beelzebul. 24:12 One of the names of the devil. Also in verse 27. The prince of demons."
And Jesus, knowing their thoughts, said to them: "Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation, and every city or house divided against itself will not stand."
If the devil drives out spirits and fights against himself, how can his kingdom stand?
If I cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul, by whom do your disciples cast them out? Therefore, they will be your judges.
But if I drive out demons by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
How can someone enter the house of a strong man and plunder his possessions, unless the strong man is first bound? Then he becomes able to plunder his house.
"Whoever is not with me is against me. And whoever does not gather with me scatters."
Therefore I tell you: every sin and insult can be forgiven to people, but the insult against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Whoever speaks against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, neither in this world nor in the world to come. The fruit reveals the truth.
To get good fruit, plant a good tree. As for the bad tree, it gives you bad fruit. Because a tree is known by its fruit.
O children of vipers, how can you speak good things while you are evil? For the mouth speaks what the heart is full of.
A righteous person brings forth what is good from their good treasure, and an evil person brings forth what is evil from the evil stored within them.
But I tell you that on the day of judgment, people will be asked about every word they said.
"And your words will decide your innocence or guilt." Jewish leaders demand proof.
Then some of the teachers of the law and Pharisees asked him, "Teacher, we want to see a miraculous sign from you."
He answered them, "This wicked and sinful generation seeks a sign to believe. And no sign will be given except the sign of the prophet Jonah."
Just as Jonah stayed in the belly of the great fish for three days and three nights, so the Son of Man will stay in the heart of the earth for three days and three nights.
The people of Nineveh will stand on the Day of Judgment against this generation, and they will condemn it because they repented when they heard Jonah's warning. And now here before you is someone greater than Jonah.
And the Queen of the South will stand #QueenOfTheSouth. 42:12 Queen of Sheba. She traveled about two thousand kilometers to hear the wisdom of God from the mouth of King Solomon. See 1 Kings 10:1-13. On the day of judgment, she will testify against this generation and condemn it. For she came from the ends of the earth to hear Solomon's wisdom. And now here before you is one greater than Solomon. The empty soul
When an impure spirit comes out of a person, it passes through dry places seeking a place to rest, but finds none,
Then he says: "I will return to the house I came from." He goes and finds the house empty, swept, and tidy.
Then he goes and brings with him seven other spirits more wicked than himself, and they enter and dwell there. Thus, the final state of that person is worse than his first state. This is what will happen with this present evil generation.» Jesus' disciples are his family
While Jesus was speaking to the crowds, his mother and brothers came and stood outside, wanting to speak to him.
Then someone said to Jesus, "Your mother and your brothers are standing outside, wanting to speak with you."
Jesus answered him, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Then he pointed with his hand to his disciples and said: "These are my mother and my brothers,
"For whoever does the will of my Father in heaven is my brother and sister and mother."
Like seeds
On that day, Jesus left the house and sat by the lakeshore.
A large crowd gathered around him. He got into a boat and sat in it, while the people stood on the shore.
And he told them many things in parables. He said to them, "A farmer went out to sow."
And while he was sowing, some of the seeds fell beside the road, and the birds came and ate them.
And some of the seeds fell on rocky ground, where there was not enough soil, so the grains grew quickly because the soil was not deep.
But when the sun rose, it burned, and because it had no roots, it withered.
And some of the seeds fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and choked the plants.
And other seeds fell on good soil, and some yielded a hundredfold, some sixtyfold, and some thirtyfold.
"Whoever has ears, let them hear." Hearing and understanding
The disciples came to him and asked, "Why do you speak to them using symbolic examples?"
Jesus answered them, "God has given you the privilege to know the secret of the kingdom of heaven, but He has not given it to them."
Because whoever owns #who owns. 12:13 Perhaps the intended meaning is "who owns understanding." It will be increased for him and will overflow from him, but whoever does not own it, what he has will be taken away from him.
Therefore I speak to them in parables; though seeing, they do not perceive. Though hearing, they do not understand.
And thus the prophecy of Isaiah applies to them: "You will hear and hear, but you will not understand. You will look and look, but you will not see."
The mind of this people has become dull, and their hearing heavy. They have closed their eyes so that they cannot see with their eyes, nor hear with their ears, nor understand with their hearts, lest they return to me and I heal them.
As for you, blessed are your eyes because they see, and your ears because they hear.
I tell you the truth, many kings and righteous people longed to see what you see but did not see it, and desired to hear what you hear but did not hear it. The meaning of the parable of the sower
So listen to the explanation of the parable of the seeds:
When a person hears the message of the kingdom and does not understand it, the evil one comes #the evil one. 19:13 That is, the devil. (Also in verse 38) And takes away the seeds sown in his heart. This is the meaning of the seeds that fell by the roadside.
As for the one who fell on the rocky ground, it is like someone who hears the word and immediately receives it with joy,
But because he has no roots in himself, he endures for a short time, and when trouble and persecution come because of the word he accepted, he quickly loses his faith.
As for the one that fell among the thorns, it is like someone who hears the word, but the worries of life and the lure of wealth choke the word, so it does not bear fruit.
As for the one who was sown on good soil, he is the one who hears the word and understands it, and indeed bears fruit. He yields a hundredfold, sixtyfold, and thirtyfold. Like wheat and weeds.
And he told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed good seeds in his field.
But while the people were sleeping, the man's enemy came and sowed weeds among the wheat, then left.
And when the wheat sprouted and formed ears, the weeds also grew.
So his servants came to him and said, "O master, did you not sow good seeds in your field? Then where did these weeds come from?"
The man answered them: "My enemy did that." So his servants asked him: "Do you want us to go and uproot it?"
The man replied: "No, because when you uproot the weeds, you might uproot the wheat along with them."
Let them grow together until the time of harvest; then I will say to the harvesters: "First, gather the weeds and bind them in bundles to be burned. But gather the wheat and store it in my barn." For example, mustard and yeast.
Then Jesus told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed that a man took and planted in his field.
It is the smallest of seeds. But when it grows, it becomes the largest of garden plants, becoming a big tree, so much so that the birds of the sky come to it and make their nests in its branches.»
And he told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast that a woman took and mixed into three measures of flour until the whole dough was leavened."
Jesus said all these things using parables. He did not speak to the people except in parables.
This is what God said through the Prophet: "I will open my mouth with parables, and I will speak of hidden things since the world was created."
Then Jesus dismissed the crowds and went into the house. His disciples came to him and said, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He said to them: "The one who sows the good seeds is the Son of Man,
The field is the world, and the good seeds are those who belong to the kingdom. As for the weeds, they are those who belong to the evil one.
And the enemy who sowed them is Satan. The harvest is the end of the world. And the harvesters are the angels.
Just as weeds are gathered and burned by fire, so will be the end of the world.
For the Son of Man will send his angels, who will gather out of his kingdom all causes of sin and all law-breakers,
Then they throw them into the blazing oven. There, people cry and grit their teeth.
Then the righteous will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears, let them hear. The parable of the treasure and the pearl
The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. A man found it and hid it again. In his joy, he went and sold all that he had and bought that field.
The kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking fine pearls.
And when he found a very precious pearl, he went and sold everything he owned and bought it. Like a fishing net.
The kingdom of heaven is like a net that was thrown into the lake and caught fish of various kinds.
And when the net was full, the fishermen pulled it to the shore. Then they sat down and began selecting the good fish and placing them in baskets, while the bad fish they threw out.
This is what will happen at the end of the world, when the angels will come and separate the wicked from the righteous,
Then the wicked are thrown into the blazing furnace. There, people weep and gnash their teeth.»
And Jesus asked his disciples, "Do you understand all these things?" They answered, "Yes."
He said to them: "Therefore, every teacher of the law who has been instructed about the kingdom of heaven is like the owner of a house who brings out of his storeroom new treasures as well as old." Jesus goes to his hometown
And when Jesus had finished telling those parables, he left that place.
And when he went to his town, he began to teach them in their synagogue. Everyone was amazed and said, "Where did this man get such wisdom and these miracles?"
Isn't he the carpenter's son? Isn't his mother Mary? Aren't his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Does he not support all his siblings among us? So where did he get everything he has?»
So that was an obstacle preventing them from accepting him. As for Jesus, he said to them: "No prophet is without honor except in his own country and in his own house!"
He did not perform many miracles there because of their lack of faith.
Herod hears about Jesus
At that time, Herod, the governor of Galilee #governor of Galilee. 1:14 literally "governor of the quarter." The Romans had divided Palestine into four provinces, so the ruler of each province was called the governor of the quarter. See Luke 3:1. About Jesus.
He said to his servants: "This is John the Baptist; he has risen from the dead, and that is why miracles are performed through him!" The Martyrdom of John the Baptist
Herod is the one who arrested John and put him in prison, and this was because of Herodias, the wife of his brother Philip,
Because John said to Herod, "It is not lawful for you to have your brother's wife."
That is why Herod wanted to kill him, but he was afraid of the people, because they considered John a prophet.
But when Herod's birthday came, Herodias's daughter danced before him and his guests, and she pleased Herod very much,
He even promised and swore to give her whatever she asked for, no matter what.
But her mother had taught her what to ask for, so she said, "Give me the head of John the Baptist here on a platter."
The king was saddened, but he ordered her request to be fulfilled because of his oath and out of respect for his guests.
So he sent someone to behead John in prison.
Then he brought his head on a plate and gave it to her, so she gave it to her mother.
Then the disciples of John came and took his body and buried it. Then they went and told Jesus what had happened. Jesus feeds more than five thousand.
When Jesus heard this, he got into a boat and went to a secluded place. But the crowds learned about it, and they came out of their towns on foot and followed him.
And when he came down to the shore, he saw a large crowd, so he felt compassion for them and healed the sick among them.
In the evening, his disciples came to him and said, "This place is isolated and the time is very late, so send the people away to go to the villages and buy food for themselves."
But Jesus said to them, "There is no need for them to go away; you give them something to eat."
So his disciples said to him, "We have here only five loaves of bread and two fish."
Then Jesus said to them, "Bring her to me."
And he commanded the people to sit on the grass. Then Jesus took the five loaves and the two fish, gave thanks to God, lifting his eyes to heaven. Then he broke them and gave the loaves to his disciples, who distributed them to the people.
So they all ate and were satisfied. And they gathered up the leftover fragments, twelve baskets full.
The number of those who ate was five thousand men, besides women and children. Jesus walks on the water.
Then Jesus asked his disciples to get into the boat and go ahead of him to the other side, while he dismissed the crowd.
After sending them away, he went up the mountain alone to pray. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The boat had reached the middle of the lake, and the waves were crashing against it violently because the wind was against the direction of the boat.
Just before dawn, Jesus came to them walking on the lake.
When his disciples saw him walking on the lake, they were terrified and said, "It is a ghost," and in their fear, they cried out.
Then Jesus immediately said to them, "Take courage, it is I; do not be afraid."
Then Peter answered him, "Lord, if it is really you, command me to come to you walking on the water."
So he said to him, "Come." Then Peter got out of the boat and walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter noticed the strong wind, he became afraid and began to sink, and he cried out, "Lord, save me."
Then Jesus immediately reached out his hand and caught him, and said to him, "You of little faith, why did you doubt?"
And when Jesus and Peter got into the boat, the wind ceased.
And those who were in the boat worshiped Jesus, saying, "Truly, you are the Son of God." Jesus heals many.
And when they crossed the lake, they reached the region of Gennesaret.
And when the inhabitants of that area recognized Jesus, they announced his coming throughout the surrounding region, and they brought to him all the sick,
And they begged him to allow them to touch the hem of his garment only. And all who touched it were healed.
God's law and people's traditions
Then some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus from Jerusalem and asked him:
Why do your students break the traditions we inherited from our ancestors? They do not wash their hands before eating.
Jesus answered them, "And why do you break God's commandment because of your traditions?"
For God commanded and said: ‹Honor your father and your mother,› #Honor … your mother. 4:15 from the Book of Exodus 20:12, and the Book of Deuteronomy 5:16. And He said: ‹Whoever curses his father or his mother shall be put to death.› #Whoever curses … shall be put to death. 4:15 from the Book of Exodus 21:17.
But you say, "Whoever says to his father or mother, 'I cannot help you, because all that I have is dedicated to the Lord,' is not obligated to honor his father or mother."
And thus you have ignored God's command because of your traditions.
O hypocrites, Isaiah was truthful when he prophesied about you and said:
This people honor me with their lips, but their heart is far from me.
Their worship is useless, because they follow teachings that are nothing but human commandments.
And Jesus called the people to him and said to them, "Listen to me and understand what I say:
It is not what enters a person's mouth that defiles them, but what comes out of their mouth; that is what defiles them.
Then the disciples came to him and said, "Do you know that the Pharisees were offended when they heard what you said?"
Jesus answered, "Every plant that my Father has not planted will be uprooted."
"Let them be, for they are blind leading the blind. And if a blind man leads another blind man, both will fall into the pit."
Then Peter answered, "Explain to us the meaning of this parable."
And Jesus said, "Have you not understood yet?"
Do you not understand that everything that enters a person's mouth goes into the stomach, and then comes out again?
But what comes out of a person's mouth originates from the heart. And this is what defiles a person.
Because from the heart come evil thoughts, murder, immorality, adultery, theft, false testimony, and insult.
These are the things that defile a person, but eating with unwashed hands does not make a person unclean.» Jesus helps a foreign woman
And Jesus left that place and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A Canaanite woman who lived in that area came to him and began to cry out, "Have mercy on me, Lord, Son of David. My daughter is possessed by an evil spirit, and she is suffering greatly."
But Jesus did not answer her a word. Then his disciples came and begged him, saying, "Send her away, for she keeps crying out after us."
He said: "I was sent only to the lost sheep of the children of Israel."
But the woman approached him, bowed before him, and said, "Lord, help me."
Jesus answered her, "It is not right to take the children's food and throw it to the dogs."
She said: "That's true, sir, but even dogs eat from what falls off their owners' tables."
Then Jesus answered her, "Woman, your faith is very great. Let it be done for you as you wish." And at that moment, her daughter was healed. Jesus heals many.
And Jesus left that place and went to an area near the Sea of Galilee. He climbed a hill and sat there.
Large crowds came to him, bringing with them the lame, the blind, the crippled, the mute, and many others who were sick. They laid them at Jesus' feet, and he healed them.
The crowds were amazed when they saw the deaf speaking, the lame walking, the paralyzed walking, and the blind seeing, and they glorified the God of Israel. Jesus feeds more than four thousand.
Then Jesus called his disciples and said, "I have compassion for these people, for they have been with me for three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, or they may faint on the way."
So his disciples said to him, "Where will we get bread enough for this crowd in such a remote place?"
Then Jesus said to them, "How many loaves do you have?" They said to him, "Seven loaves and a few small fish."
So Jesus commanded the people to sit on the ground.
He took the seven loaves and the fish, gave thanks, and broke the loaves, and gave them to the disciples who distributed them to everyone.
So everyone ate and was satisfied. Then they gathered what was left of the fragments, and there were seven full baskets.
The number of eaters was four thousand men, excluding women and children.
And when Jesus sent the crowds away, he got into a boat and went to the region of Magdala.
Jewish leaders test Jesus
The Pharisees and the Sadducees came to Jesus to test him, and they asked him to give them a sign to prove God's support for him.
Jesus answered them, "In the evening you say, 'It will be fair weather, for the sky is red.'"
And early in the morning, you say: "Today will be stormy, because the sky is red and gloomy." You are good at interpreting weather signs, but you do not understand the times you are living in!
This wicked, sinful generation seeks a sign to believe. And no sign will be given except the sign of Jonah." Then Jesus left them and went away. Lack of understanding
And Jesus' disciples crossed to the other side of the lake, but they forgot to bring bread.
And Jesus said to them, "Beware and be on your guard against the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
Then the disciples began to talk among themselves, saying, "But we did not bring any bread!"
Jesus knew what they were saying and said to them, "O you of little faith, why are you arguing among yourselves about having no bread?
Have you not realized yet? Do you not remember the five loaves for the five thousand, and how many baskets you gathered of the leftovers?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four thousand, and how many baskets you gathered of the leftovers?
Why don't you understand that I was not talking to you about ordinary bread, but I was warning you to guard yourselves against the yeast of the Pharisees and the Sadducees.»
Then his disciples understood that he was not warning them about the yeast of bread, but about the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter declares that Jesus is the Christ.
When Jesus came to the region of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, "Who do people say that I am, the Son of Man?"
And he answered his disciples: "Some say you are John the Baptist, others say you are Elijah, and others say you are Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
So he said to them, "And you, who do you say I am?"
Simon Peter answered, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered him, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah, for this was not revealed to you by man, but by my Father in heaven.
And I say to you, you are Peter, #Peter. 18:16 From the Greek "Petros," meaning "rock." And on this rock I will build my church, and the gates of hell #the gates of hell. 18:16 That is, the power of death. You will not be able to overcome it.
And I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven; whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.”
Then he strongly warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks about the inevitability of his death.
From that time on, Jesus began to explain to his disciples that he must go to the city of Jerusalem, and that he would suffer many things from the elders, chief priests, and teachers of the law. He must also be killed and rise on the third day.
But Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him, saying, "God forbid, Lord! This shall never happen to you!"
Then Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me because you do not have in mind the concerns of God, but merely human concerns."
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "If anyone wants to come with me, he must deny himself, take up the cross given to him, and follow me."
Whoever wants to save their life will lose it. But whoever loses their life for my sake will find it.
What good is it for a man to gain the whole world, yet lose himself? And what can a man do to regain his life?
For the Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and He will reward each one according to his works.
Truly, I say to you, among those standing here are some who will not taste death before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.»
Jesus with Moses and Elijah
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and his brother John, and led them up a high mountain to be alone.
And while they were there, Jesus' appearance changed and he began to shine like the sun, and his clothes became white as light.
Suddenly, Moses and Elijah appeared before the disciples, and they were talking with Jesus.
Then Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, how good it is for us to be here! If you wish, I will put up three shelters here, one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And while Peter was speaking, a shining cloud overshadowed them, and a voice came out of it saying: "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to him."
When the disciples heard this, they were terrified and fell to the ground on their faces.
Jesus came near and touched them, saying, "Get up, do not be afraid."
And when they looked around, they saw no one except Jesus.
And as they were coming down the mountain, Jesus instructed them, saying, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
And his disciples asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?" #Elijah … first. 10:17 Elijah was one of God's prophets around 850 BC. The Jews were expecting his coming based on Malachi 4:5-6.
Jesus answered them, "Yes, Elijah is coming to restore all things to their original state."
But I tell you that Elijah has already come, and people did not recognize him, but treated him however they wished. And the Son of Man will also be treated that way by them.»
Then his disciples understood that he was speaking about John the Baptist. Jesus casts out an evil spirit from a boy.
And when they returned to the crowd, a man came to Jesus and bowed down before him.
He said: "Have mercy on my son, O Lord, for he suffers from epilepsy and is in great pain. He often falls into fire or water.
And I brought him to your disciples, but they could not heal him.»
Then Jesus said, "You unbelieving and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? How long shall I bear with you?" Then he said to the man, "Bring your son here to me."
Jesus commanded the evil spirit to come out of him, and the boy was healed immediately.
Then his disciples came to him privately and asked, "Why couldn't we drive it out?"
Jesus answered them, "Because of your little faith. Truly I tell you, if you have faith as small as a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move; nothing will be impossible for you.
But this kind does not come out except by prayer and fasting." Jesus predicts the approach of his death.
And while they were traveling together in Galilee, Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man is about to be delivered into the hands of men."
And they will kill him. But on the third day he will rise from the dead." The disciples were very sad. Temple tax
And when Jesus entered Capernaum, he came to Peter, who was collecting the two-drachma temple tax, and they asked him, "Does your teacher not pay the two-drachma tax?"
Then Peter answered them, "Yes, he does." Then Peter went home. Jesus began to speak to him and said, "Tell me, Simon, from whom do kings collect tribute and taxes? From their own children, or from others?"
Then Peter answered, "They gather it from other peoples." Jesus said, "Then the children are exempt from it."
But so as not to cause them any trouble, go to the lake and cast your fishing rod. Then take the first fish you catch and open its mouth. You will find a coin worth four dirhams inside. Take it and give it to them on my behalf and yours.»
